# Beat: guided walk through bob/logfile against alice/app.desc.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc
#
# The root type is not in alice/app.desc — it is Bob's own log envelope,
# which reproto recovered no schema for.  The session opens untyped.
# This beat names the structure by hand, enables heat cues, and follows
# the cues as far as alice/app.desc can take them.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The logfile, app.desc

steps:
  - text: |
      This is bob/logfile opened
      against the descriptors in alice/app.desc.

      bob/logfile is a larger protobuf than bob/capture.
      The root type is unknown — no schema in alice/app.desc
      matches the envelope.  What we see is raw field numbers.

      👋 Press space to step.

    node: /
    select: true

  - text: |
      Let's fold the top level nodes to get a clearer view.
      Notice the annotation on the 4th node: TRUNCATED_MESSAGE

      The process was probably killed in flight and the logfile misses 1024 bytes.
      This explains why protoc was unable to parse it.

      👋 Press space to step.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    select: true
    search: TRUNCATED_MESSAGE

  - text: |
      Let's unfold to depth 2.

      The top-level fields each contain similar submessages.
      They look like log entries — one per RPC call.

      👋 Press space to step.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "1 [{]  #@ message"

  - text: |
      Let's name this structure using the proposed prefilled command below.

      It declares a new synthetic type "Entry", marks field 1 as
      repeated and gives it the field name "entries".
      
      👋 Press Enter to commit the type override.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "1 [{]  #@ message"
    command: override /:1 --as-new Entry --cardinality repeated --field-name entries
    advance_when:
      - type: /1 Entry

  - text: |
      👍 Good, the Entry type override is in place.
       
      To go futher, let's enable protolens heat cues.
      Protolens will tell us where in the protobuf it recognizes known types.
      Heat cues show as green/amber/blue annotations.

      👋 Press `i` to enable heat cues.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "entries [{]  #@ repeated Entry"
    advance_when:
      - heat_cues: findings

  - text: |
      We see that field 5 of the first entry has a bright heat cue.
      Hover over it. Notice it proposes two matching types with identical score.
      
      Let's pick SearchTextResponse, it feels right and corresponds to what was in capture.
      
      👋 Double-click on the heat-cue, then double-click on SearchTextResponse.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/4
    select: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/4 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextResponse

  - text: |
      👍 Good, the SearchTextResponse override is in place.

      Now hover over the amber heat-cue of field 4.
      It proposes a single matching type.
      
      Let's accept SearchTextRequest, it feels right.
      
      👋 Double-click on the heat-cue, then double-click on SearchTextRequest.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/3
    select: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/3 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      👍 OK, SearchTextRequest is also in place.

      What about field 2?
      It looks like the name of the logged RPC.
      Let's just name the field "rpc".

      👋 Press Enter to commit the prefilled command.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/2
    select: true
    command: override Entry:2 --field-name rpc
    advance_when:
      - field_name: /1/2 rpc

  - text: |
      Now what about field 1?
      Let's hover over the heat cue...
      Hmm... Timestamp is probably right, for a log entry...
      
      👋 Double-click on the heat-cue, then double-click on Timestamp.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /1/1
    select: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/1 google.protobuf.Timestamp

  - text: |
      👌 Perfect

      Now let's have a look at the 3rd node.
      Fields 6 and 7 heat cues show deeply negative scores.😨
      And hovering above them gives nothing convincing. 🤔🤔🤔

      👋 Press space to step.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    select: true

  - text: |
      🤔 Nothing fits...

      Maybe the app.desc descriptors are not complete...
      SearchTextRequest/Response are from the googleapis descriptors.
      Let's try to re-open bob/logfile against the full googleapis.desc,
      instead of app.desc.

      👋 Press space to step.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    select: true

  - text: |
      ✋ Before that, let's save our current work:
      keep a copy of the overrides we have defined.

      👋 Press Enter to save our overrides.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    command: save-overrides alice/overrides
    advance_when:
      - file_exists: alice/overrides

  - text: |
      Now we can press Enter to quit.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    command: quit
